Theorem RL-103: Engine composition acyclicity

  Preconditions:
    - the eight engines at aims-proof/checker/src/engine compose via
      the top-level dispatch loop in aims-proof/checker/src/checker.rs
      per the proof-checker design sec-Architecture-Sketch
    - each engine implements the Engine trait at
      aims-proof/checker/src/engine/mod.rs with stateless discharge
      per the gate-vs-implementation split Option A
    - each engine.dispatch consumes only the Theorem argument and
      returns an EngineResult; no engine reads or writes shared
      mutable state across calls
    - the call graph over engine modules is the set of pairs
      (engines[i], engines[j]) where engines[i].dispatch transitively
      calls engines[j].dispatch
    - acyclicity means the call graph is a directed acyclic graph;
      the dispatch loop in checker.rs is the only composition site

  Soundness property:
    Forall i, j in 0 to 7 with i not equal to j.
      engines[i].dispatch does not call engines[j].dispatch;
      every engine's dispatch returns a verdict computed solely
        from the theorem argument and from engine-local state;
      the call graph over engine modules is empty
        (no edges; trivially acyclic).

  Proof obligation:
    Structural induction over the engine inventory; case analysis
    over every engine pair.

      Base case: zero engines.
        Call graph has zero nodes and zero edges; vacuously acyclic.

      Inductive step: extending the inventory by one engine preserves
      acyclicity.
        Assume the inventory of N engines composes acyclically.
        For the (N + 1) engine e:
          (a) e.dispatch is stateless w.r.t. other engines per
              the gate-vs-implementation split Option A; this is a code-level invariant
              enforced by the Engine trait signature (no engine
              registry argument; no other-engine references).
          (b) e does not import any other engine module per the
              aims-proof/checker/src/engine/mod.rs structure (each
              engine is a leaf submodule; none re-export or invoke
              siblings).
          (c) the dispatch loop in checker.rs is the composition
              site; checker.rs calls each engine exactly once per
              theorem; no engine calls checker.rs.
        Therefore extending the inventory by e adds zero edges to
        the call graph; the graph remains acyclic.

      Closed-world coverage over the eight engines:
        case_analysis: leaf — no engine invocations.
        refinement: leaf — no engine invocations.
        rc_counting: leaf — no engine invocations.
        lattice: leaf — no engine invocations.
        monotonicity: leaf — no engine invocations.
        fixpoint: leaf — no engine invocations.
        structural_induction: leaf — no engine invocations.
        interprocedural_summary: leaf — no engine invocations.

      Composition site (checker.rs):
        dispatch_theorem walks the per-category engine list and calls
        engine.dispatch once per engine; no engine calls back into
        dispatch_theorem; no engine calls another engine.
        The call graph is rooted at check_proof_file and is a tree
        of depth at most 2 (check_proof_file -> dispatch_theorem ->
        engine.dispatch); engine.dispatch is always a leaf.

    Lattice validation:
      Acyclicity is a structural property of the call graph; lattice
      dimensions do not participate. The kernel invariant under
      verification is that the engine inventory composes only via the
      dispatch loop per the gate-vs-implementation split Option A.

    Engines dispatched:
      structural_induction (over the engine call-graph DAG; per-node
                            leaf verification)
      case_analysis (per-engine-pair enumeration over the inventory;
                     leaf-vs-internal classification per engine module)

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: 01A bootstrap discharged via §01A Implementation Items (engine constructive verification + Lean 4 cross-validation)
